E+= [P('4.6 What a shutdown is worth',H2),P('Whatever its cause, the shutdown inning matters for winning. After an opportunity, a scoreless inning raises the pitching team\'s win probability by about 5.0 points on average; allowing a run lowers it by about 13.1 points, an 18-point swing (Figure 10). The swing is similar across innings 1-9 and larger in extra innings.')]
E+= [fig('fig10_win_probability_swing.png','Figure 10. Average win-probability change for the pitching team across an opportunity half-inning, by outcome.',5.4)]
E+= [P('5. Discussion',H1),
P('<b>What SIP is.</b> SIP is a transparent, easy-to-audit descriptive statistic. It tells you how often a pitcher actually protected a lead he was just handed, and it ties to a real swing in win probability. For fans, broadcasters and front offices that want to talk about shutdown innings, SIP gives the phrase a number. It can appear in a box score as a simple line (for example "SD 3/4") at essentially no cost, because every needed input already exists in a play-by-play record.'),
P('<b>What SIP is not.</b> The folklore that shutdown innings are a distinct psychological test does not survive the data. The league scores the same share of innings whether or not the home team has just scored, the amount scored does not matter, and the pitcher-specific part of SIP does not repeat. Its predictive value is general talent: pitchers with high SIP are the pitchers with high scoreless rates everywhere. We therefore recommend reporting SIP together with a context benchmark (expected SIP) and treating small samples with caution: at 40 opportunities a pitcher\'s SIP has a standard error of about 7 points, larger than the true spread among pitchers (about 2 points).'),
P('<b>Coasting.</b> Large leads give a small, measurable decline (about %.1f points within pitchers). That is consistent with pitchers throwing more strikes and trading some run prevention for efficiency when the game is not close, but the data cannot separate that from game-state selection and defensive alignments.'%abs(bl['diff_pts']))]
E+= [P('6. Limitations',H1),
P('Credit is assigned to the pitcher who starts the half-inning, so relievers who inherit trouble are not charged for a predecessor\'s runners and a starter pulled mid-inning may be blamed or credited for what happens after him. SIP+ adjusts for opposing offense, park era, inning and the extra-inning rule but not for the pitcher\'s own defense, pitch quality or handedness matchups. The empirical-Bayes variance estimate assumes a roughly normal distribution of talent. The win-probability model is empirical and ignores base-out states inside innings. The reliability comparison between SIP and the baseline is not exactly like-for-like because the baseline pools about 2.6 times as many half-innings. The sample is regular-season, 2000-2025; 2020 is a 60-game season.')]
